    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Singapore joined the Federation of Malaysia on 16 September 1963 and was expelled from it two years later: on 9 August 1965 the Malaysian Parliament voted 126 to 0 to separate it, leaving a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> without a hinterland.</li><li>Growth averaged about 6 percent a year from 1965 to 1995 and about 8 percent through the 1980s and 1990s, making Singapore one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-asian-tigers">Four Asian Tigers</a>, the only Asian state with a AAA rating from all the major agencies and the country with the highest PPP-adjusted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> per head in the world.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-action-party">People's Action Party</a> has won every election since 1959; the Workers' Party took 10 of 93 seats in 2020, when Pritam Singh became the first de jure leader of the opposition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lawrence-wong">Lawrence Wong</a>, the first prime minister born after independence, took office on 15 May 2024.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At independence Singapore had only two infantry regiments commanded by British officers, so the government asked commanders of the Israel Defense Forces to build the Singapore Armed Forces from scratch; its conscription and reserve system still follows the Israeli model.</p></aside>
    </section>

